- Strong scaling keeps the workload fixed; weak scaling grows the workload with the cluster.
- Step time includes compute, communication and waiting.
- Useful throughput must also account for rollback and recovery.
- Scaling efficiency falls when overhead grows faster than compute time shrinks.
- The best cluster size depends on both cost per useful step and the value of finishing sooner.

Sources

- NVIDIA, NCCL collective operations: https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/usage/collectives.html
- NVIDIA, NCCL RAS documentation: https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/troubleshooting/ras.html
- PyTorch, DistributedDataParallel documentation: https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.parallel.DistributedDataParallel.html
- PyTorch, distributed communication documentation: https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/distributed.html

